1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,988
There is no rock on Earth as
old as the Earth.

2
00:00:04,038 --> 00:00:08,774
The first ones were melted, buried and
remade billions of years ago.

3
00:00:08,824 --> 00:00:13,419
So how do we know the planet is four and
a half billion years old?

4
00:00:13,933 --> 00:00:19,294
The answer came from a rock that fell from the
sky — and from a chemist who could

5
00:00:19,344 --> 00:00:24,505
not measure it, because everything he touched
was poisoned with lead. The air.

6
00:00:24,555 --> 00:00:26,977
The water. The dust on his own hands.

7
00:00:27,467 --> 00:00:32,105
To find the age of the Earth, he had to build
the cleanest room on the planet.

8
00:00:32,155 --> 00:00:36,852
And to explain the lead, he had to take on one
of the biggest industries in the

9
00:00:36,902 --> 00:00:37,213
world.

10
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:39,119
He won both fights.

11
00:00:39,633 --> 00:00:42,261
This is how we learned how
old the Earth is.

12
00:00:45,367 --> 00:00:51,684
The first scientific answer came in 1862, from
the most famous physicist in Britain.

13
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:57,732
William Thomson — later Lord Kelvin — assumed
the Earth had started as a ball of

14
00:00:57,782 --> 00:01:00,802
molten rock and had been
cooling ever since.

15
00:01:00,852 --> 00:01:04,430
Measure how fast heat leaks out
of the ground today,

16
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:06,942
and you can run the clock backwards.

17
00:01:07,433 --> 00:01:10,793
His answer: twenty to four
hundred million years.

18
00:01:10,843 --> 00:01:13,576
Later he narrowed it to twenty to forty.

19
00:01:13,626 --> 00:01:19,073
He was wrong by a factor of a hundred — and
almost nobody could argue with him.

20
00:01:19,567 --> 00:01:20,959
Geologists hated it.

21
00:01:21,009 --> 00:01:26,873
Their layers of rock, their canyons cut grain by
grain, seemed to need far longer.

22
00:01:26,923 --> 00:01:32,282
Darwin hated it more: twenty million years
was nowhere near enough time for

23
00:01:32,332 --> 00:01:33,003
evolution.

24
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:35,672
Others tried different clocks.

25
00:01:35,722 --> 00:01:41,820
The Irish physicist John Joly asked how long the
rivers would need to carry all the

26
00:01:41,870 --> 00:01:47,523
salt that is now in the sea. His answer, in
1899: about ninety million years.

27
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:50,415
Every clock gave a different answer.

28
00:01:50,465 --> 00:01:56,022
And all of them were wrong for the same reason:
each assumed a process that had run

29
00:01:56,072 --> 00:01:57,779
steadily, and nothing had.

30
00:02:00,867 --> 00:02:04,969
The way out came from somewhere
nobody was looking.

31
00:02:05,019 --> 00:02:10,992
In 1896, Henri Becquerel found that uranium
gives off radiation by itself.

32
00:02:11,042 --> 00:02:17,505
Within a few years, Ernest Rutherford showed
what that meant: atoms were falling

33
00:02:17,555 --> 00:02:17,993
apart.

34
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:21,382
That broke Kelvin's calculation twice.

35
00:02:21,432 --> 00:02:24,930
Radioactive decay heats the
Earth from inside,

36
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:28,325
so it cools far more slowly
than he assumed.

37
00:02:28,375 --> 00:02:31,642
And it gave geologists a new
kind of clock.

38
00:02:32,133 --> 00:02:36,821
Uranium decays, through a long
chain of steps, into lead,

39
00:02:36,871 --> 00:02:43,387
at a rate that never changes — not with heat,
not with pressure, not with time.

40
00:02:43,437 --> 00:02:49,454
Half of any uranium-238 turns into lead in
four and a half billion years.

41
00:02:49,967 --> 00:02:54,176
So if a crystal locked in uranium when
it formed, and no lead,

42
00:02:54,226 --> 00:02:57,129
then the lead inside it now
is a stopwatch.

43
00:02:57,179 --> 00:03:01,182
Count the atoms, and you know how
long it has been running.

44
00:03:01,667 --> 00:03:07,928
In 1907, the American chemist Bertram Boltwood
tried it on real rocks and got ages

45
00:03:07,978 --> 00:03:10,007
of up to two billion years.

46
00:03:10,057 --> 00:03:14,779
By the 1940s, the oldest rocks pointed
to about three billion.

47
00:03:14,829 --> 00:03:21,013
The Earth had to be older still — but how much
older, no rock on Earth could say.

48
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:27,957
Remember: no rock on Earth goes
back to the beginning.

49
00:03:28,007 --> 00:03:33,672
To find the starting line, you need material
that formed with the Earth and was

50
00:03:33,722 --> 00:03:35,119
never touched again.

51
00:03:35,633 --> 00:03:41,583
Meteorites are exactly that: leftovers from the
same cloud that built the planets.

52
00:03:41,633 --> 00:03:47,291
In 1948, a young chemist in Chicago named
Clair Patterson was given the job of

53
00:03:47,341 --> 00:03:49,559
measuring the lead inside them.

54
00:03:50,067 --> 00:03:53,582
It should have taken months. It
took seven years.

55
00:03:53,632 --> 00:03:59,111
Every time he measured, he found far more
lead than could possibly be there.

56
00:03:59,161 --> 00:04:05,150
The extra lead was coming from the laboratory
itself — from the air, the glassware,

57
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:06,605
the dust, the paint.

58
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:11,974
So Patterson built one of the first clean
rooms in science. Filtered air.

59
00:04:12,024 --> 00:04:16,358
Surfaces scrubbed with acid. Chemicals
distilled again and again.

60
00:04:16,408 --> 00:04:21,417
Anyone entering had to be stripped of lead,
down to the dust on their skin.

61
00:04:21,933 --> 00:04:25,609
In 1953, working at Argonne
National Laboratory,

62
00:04:25,659 --> 00:04:31,585
he finally measured clean lead from a
meteorite that had struck Arizona fifty

63
00:04:31,635 --> 00:04:36,630
thousand years before. His result: four
and a half billion years.

64
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:42,063
Published in 1956: four point five five,
give or take seventy million.

65
00:04:42,567 --> 00:04:48,721
Seventy years later, the accepted age is four
point five four billion years.

66
00:04:48,771 --> 00:04:51,007
His number has barely moved.

67
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:57,623
But Patterson could not stop thinking
about the contamination.

68
00:04:57,673 --> 00:05:00,217
Where did all that lead in
the air come from?

69
00:05:00,733 --> 00:05:06,219
He started measuring lead everywhere — in
seawater, in ancient sediments,

70
00:05:06,269 --> 00:05:12,286
in layers of ice in Greenland. The pattern was
brutal. Old ice held almost none.

71
00:05:12,336 --> 00:05:18,353
Recent ice held many times more, and the
steepest rise had come in just the last

72
00:05:18,403 --> 00:05:19,263
few decades.

73
00:05:19,767 --> 00:05:24,588
1923 was the year tetraethyl lead was
first sold as a petrol additive.

74
00:05:24,638 --> 00:05:26,536
It stopped engines knocking.

75
00:05:26,586 --> 00:05:31,407
And every car on Earth had been spraying
lead into the air ever since.

76
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:37,392
In 1965 Patterson published it: the lead in
modern people was far above natural

77
00:05:37,442 --> 00:05:40,409
levels. The fuel industry
pushed back hard.

78
00:05:40,459 --> 00:05:46,021
His funding was cut, and he was left off a
national committee on lead pollution.

79
00:05:46,533 --> 00:05:47,495
He kept going.

80
00:05:47,545 --> 00:05:50,458
In 1970 America passed the Clean Air Act,

81
00:05:50,508 --> 00:05:55,156
and leaded petrol was phased out over
the next twenty-five years.

82
00:05:55,206 --> 00:05:59,348
Lead in Americans' blood fell by
more than three quarters.

83
00:05:59,833 --> 00:06:05,367
The last country on Earth stopped selling
leaded petrol for cars in 2021.

84
00:06:08,467 --> 00:06:14,479
Today the oldest pieces of the solar system are
dated to four billion five hundred

85
00:06:14,529 --> 00:06:16,696
and sixty-seven million years.

86
00:06:16,746 --> 00:06:21,649
The oldest crystal found on Earth, a
zircon from Western Australia,

87
00:06:21,699 --> 00:06:24,384
is four point four billion years old.

88
00:06:24,900 --> 00:06:28,454
And the Earth itself: four point
five four billion,

89
00:06:28,504 --> 00:06:34,179
give or take about one per cent — read from the
lead inside a rock that fell from

90
00:06:34,229 --> 00:06:34,744
the sky.

91
00:06:35,233 --> 00:06:39,575
Clair Patterson found the age of the
world by learning to see lead.

92
00:06:39,625 --> 00:06:41,870
And then he got the lead out of it.

93
00:06:42,367 --> 00:06:49,501
The last digit is still being argued over. The
first ones have not moved since 1956.
